'''Map image-space hand landmarks to stage-space wrist positions.

Converts MediaPipe image-space hand landmarks (normalized to the camera frame)
to a 3D position in OpenXR stage space, so hands have a meaningful placement in
the virtual scene rather than being pinned at the origin.

Lateral placement (X/Y) comes from the wrist\'s position in the camera image.
Depth (Z) is estimated from the hand\'s *apparent size*: a hand closer to the
camera spans more of the frame. We deliberately do NOT use the wrist landmark\'s
image-space ``z`` for depth — in MediaPipe ``hand_landmarks`` the wrist (index 0)
is the depth origin, so its ``z`` is always ~0 and never reflects forward/back
motion. Apparent size is the usable monocular depth cue (TDD §11 / Future Work:
"better depth estimation from hand bbox area").

    image (0,0) top-left  ->  stage (+x, +y)  top-right (mirrored)
    image (1,1) bot-right ->  stage (-x, -y)  bot-left  (mirrored)
    image center          ->  stage_center
    larger apparent hand  ->  more negative stage z (forward, toward the scene)
    smaller apparent hand ->  more positive stage z (back, toward the user)

X is mirrored so the user\'s right hand maps to stage +X (user\'s right).
Y is flipped so raising a hand maps to stage +Y (up).

When MediaPipe\'s metric world landmarks are available, ``estimate_hand_placement``
replaces both heuristics above with a pinhole camera model: the hand\'s metric
3D shape is placed where it projects onto the observed image landmarks, which
yields its camera-space position (lateral and depth) in meters. Depth maps 1:1
into stage space; sideways and vertical motion is amplified by
``WorkspaceConfig.reach_gain`` so the hand can sweep the whole view. The
palm-width model above remains the fallback.
'''
